{"author":{"id":"417295a7cc0952a9a38b67be3a894daed91f9069f7407e038d12a4f9b9a8451e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792345764,"nonce":"q9nR+gJtqEG1/M0lAxTFffSdilE=","title":"A collection inside a native-made call frees values the native holds","message":"Array.map and the other natives that take a function keep results in Rust locals while vm.call runs the callback. vm.call runs a nested fiber, and a poll in that fiber could start a collection, which could not see the locals. Reproducer, under HeapConfig{stress:true}: [1,2,3,4,5,6].map(x -\u003e { var s = 0; for (i in 0...3) { s += i; } return {a: x, b: [x, x + 1], c: 'v' + x}; }) panicked in rayscript-gc's mark (a freed object's lines were reused). It passed before the Immix swap only because freed memory stayed intact, and callbacks without a call or loop never poll.\n\nFix: poll skips the collection while Vm.nested \u003e 0. The collection happens at the next poll of the outer fiber. Cost: a callback that allocates without end cannot be collected until it returns; the heap cap still stops it.","files":null}]}